---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { stats } from "../lib/data";
const f = (n: number) => n.toLocaleString("en");
import { statSync } from "node:fs";
const dumpKB = Math.round(statSync("public/export/products.json").size / 1024).toLocaleString("en");
---
<Base title="How this works" active="about">
  <div style="max-width:640px">
    <h1 style="font-size:18px">How this works</h1>
    <p>HackerProducts answers one question for each product: why do Hacker News people use this, and who says so? It is a prototype built from one year of comments ({stats.window}).</p>
    <h2>Pipeline</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><b>Ingest.</b> {f(stats.comments)} comments from the open-index/hacker-news dataset and the Algolia HN API.</li>
      <li><b>Prefilter.</b> Rules keep comments with recommendation phrasing ("I use", "switched to", "highly recommend"), links to non-news sites, or a place in an Ask HN thread about tools: {f(stats.candidates)} candidates.</li>
      <li><b>Extract.</b> A model labels the highest-priority candidates ({f(stats.labeled)} so far, {f(stats.with_product)} of which name a product): each product named, the commenter's stance, a verbatim quote, what they use it for, alternatives, and whether they built it. The first 75,000 were labeled by Claude Sonnet and the rest by Claude Haiku with a compact prompt, after a 500-comment check against Claude Opus showed them close (F1 about 87% and 86%).</li>
      <li><b>Resolve.</b> Names are merged by link domain or GitHub repo first, then by normalized name; Sonnet reviews near-duplicates ("Postgres" and "PostgreSQL").</li>
      <li><b>Score.</b> One vote per HN user per product, and comments by the product's own makers are excluded. Lists rank by distinct recommenders, with warnings shown beside each product. AI models are ranked separately from other tools.</li>
      <li><b>Synthesize.</b> For the most-recommended products, Claude Opus groups quotes into praise and caveat themes and writes the two-sentence verdict. Any clause without a citation to a real comment is rejected.</li>
    </ol>
    <h2>What to keep in mind</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Quotes are verbatim and link to the comment. "Who recommends it" uses only what the commenter said about themselves.</li>
      <li>Only the {f(stats.labeled)} highest-priority of {f(stats.candidates)} candidate comments have been labeled so far, so counts are lower than the real totals.</li>
      <li>Labels come from a model and are sometimes wrong, and products with common names can be mixed up.</li>
    </ul>
    <h2>Keyboard</h2>
    <p><kbd>j</kbd>/<kbd>k</kbd> move through lists, <kbd>o</kbd> opens, <kbd>/</kbd> searches, <kbd>?</kbd> shows everything.</p>
    <h2 id="privacy">Privacy</h2>
    <p>No accounts and no cookies. The only outside request is Cloudflare's cookieless Web Analytics beacon (static.cloudflareinsights.com), which counts page views; product icons and everything else are served from this site.</p>
    <h2 id="data">Data</h2>
    <p>Comments come from the <a href="https://huggingface.co/datasets/open-index/hacker-news">open-index/hacker-news</a> dataset (ODC-BY license) and the <a href="https://hn.algolia.com/api">HN Algolia API</a>. Quotes are short excerpts, each linked to its comment on news.ycombinator.com. HackerProducts is an independent project, not affiliated with Y Combinator or Hacker News.</p>
    <p>Everything is static. Each list has a small "Download JSON" button. The full dump of all ranked products is <a href="/export/products.json" download="hackerproducts-products.json">products.json</a> ({dumpKB} KB), and each product's data is at <code>/export/p/&lt;slug&gt;.json</code>.</p>
  </div>
</Base>
